WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.360
How do you guys
feel like we get --

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.740
we get ourselves in the door?

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.380
How do we get ourselves
in the door more?

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.340
I don't know, man.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:14.040
You know I sold a show, I sold
a semi-autobiographical sitcom,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.840
and I sold a dramedy.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.150
And the sitcom
crashed and burned

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.660
because they did
not want to tell

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.970
a mainstream disabled story.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.550
They wanted those
tropes, so I was

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.130
hired to make a
really fun, sassy,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.890
like Sex in the
City type of comedy.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.380
And I had producers
actually tell me

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.020
that if my character
was too successful

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.540
I'd make normal people feel bad.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.660
She was stripped of
her love triangle,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.770
and instead was pining for
someone she was invisible to.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.700
I asked them to not
mention the disability.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.990
I wanted the character
just to live,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.090
and be disabled because I was.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.450
And then later in the series,
when the plot dictated,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.400
we would discuss the disability.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.710
They literally
had her screaming,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.910
I have cerebral palsy,
off the bow of a ship.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.080
Like an homage to a really
old, unfunny Titanic joke.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.330
It was so hard for
me because there

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.440
was no other disabled writer.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.710
So when I would tell
them things were ableist,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.620
things were inspiration
porn, they didn't believe me.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.450
They thought that I was just
trying to dictate storylines.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.000
And they couldn't
understand what

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.690
it meant to be offensive
to disabled people,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.430
because what they
were forcing on

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.850
me were the stories that
had always been told.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.280
So I think the biggest part
of getting through the door

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.730
is accepting the fact that
just creating your own stuff

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.270
is not enough.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.950
We need disabled people on
both sides of the camera,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.230
because it's just
too much pressure

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.060
to be the only
person in the room,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.670
and have to be like educator,
advocate, and artist.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.990
Right, to your story
Maysoon, I have

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.460
quite a few of those as well.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.770
I think one of them was
during a four camera shoot,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.700
and one of the network execs,
said about my character,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.110
who had nothing -- my character
had nothing to do with

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.900
my dwarfism at all.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.700
And so their response was,
can't we play with him somehow?

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.190
I didn't understand what that
meant, but I knew it was bad.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.840
I remember you telling me
that you had to tell people

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.497
to stop having your
character bite butts.

00:02:24.497 --> 00:02:25.080
Yeah that's --

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.788
The thing that they
always had you doing.

00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:28.015
-- That's a whole other line.

00:02:28.015 --> 00:02:29.640
I've actually had to
put in my contract

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.150
that I will not bite
someone on the butt.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.810
Any advice for young
folks coming in?

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.970
My advice for young
folks coming in.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.490
You have to be better than
everyone else, I'm sorry.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.040
You have to work harder,
you have to rehearse more,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.590
you have to be better,
you can't be just as good.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.840
It's the only way you're
going to get through the door

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:49.770
and shine.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.910
Do more than one thing.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:55.380
So like I'm a comic, but I
also commentate on the news,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.560
I write graphic novels,
I do voice-overs.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.370
Figure out at least two other
things that you're good at that

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.780
make money.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.800
Because you need to make
money in order to pursue this.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.740
And if you're trying to
make money off of this

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:11.950
it will kill you.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.390
So find a way to make
money, in the genre,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.510
that doesn't include
carrying 50-pound scripts.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.040
So that people know who you are.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.250
People trust you, and people
want to work with you.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.260
And don't be afraid to
ask for accommodations.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.940
Like Eryn said, if
you have a team,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.210
have them pack it
into your contract.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.270
If you don't have a
team, don't be afraid.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.168
Again, all of Hollywood
is high maintenance.

00:03:39.168 --> 00:03:40.710
You're not going to
be the person who

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.350
breaks the $100 million budget.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.660
Don't be afraid to ask
for the things you need,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.890
you're worth it.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.320
I think find and
follow your passion,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.690
because it's the only thing
that will propel one through.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.520
None of us do this
for the money.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.490
We do it because we love
it, because we can't

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.330
imagine doing anything else.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.940
I do it for the money, 100%.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.130
I think that's bad,
but I swear to god,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.557
I do it for the
money it's not --

00:04:09.557 --> 00:04:10.140
No, you don't.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:10.770
You love what you do.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.380
I do.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.270
It's the red carpet, like
my stylist got me this suit.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.690
I swear to you, I do
it for the gift bags.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.100
Well, I can't really
argue with that.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.240
You know it's true.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.320
I guess I would say, follow
your passion and be authentic.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.912
So Maysoon, you hit it.

00:04:28.912 --> 00:04:30.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.800
I will add one other
thing to everything

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.560
you guys said, because you
kind of all covered my answer.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.870
In this industry you
have to find your voice,

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:42.140
and find that space that you
fill that no one else does.

